## 5. How to invent something actually new

I would choose one focused research problem and create an algorithm around it.

### Proposed invention: Godavari Adaptive Safety Intelligence (GASI)

Step 1 — Live Observation

Density, movement, arrivals, routes, closures, responder availability

Step 2 — Probabilistic Digital Twin

Estimate current state and possible future states with uncertainty

Step 3 — Counterfactual Simulation

Evaluate multiple proposed interventions across plausible scenarios

Step 4 — Safety & Fairness Evaluation

Reject options that overload another corridor, block accessibility or exceed uncertainty limits

Step 5 — Human Authorization

A qualified operator selects and approves an intervention

Step 6 — Measure and Learn

Compare actual subsequent observations with the predicted effects

Repeat continuously

The potentially novel part would not be the digital twin alone. It would be the risk-aware, uncertainty-aware, fairness-constrained intervention selection and real-time feedback algorithm designed specifically for temporary mass pilgrimages.

You could evaluate this against baseline approaches using simulated scenarios: static thresholds, shortest-path rerouting and conventional capacity-based recommendations.

### A research objective

Minimize a combination of:

\\[ J = w_1R\_{\text{crowd}} +w_2T\_{\text{response}} +w_3C\_{\text{secondary}} +w_4I\_{\text{inequity}} +w_5U\_{\text{model}} \\]

Where:

- \\(R\_{\text{crowd}}\\): estimated crowd safety risk
- \\(T\_{\text{response}}\\): response delay
- \\(C\_{\text{secondary}}\\): congestion caused elsewhere
- \\(I\_{\text{inequity}}\\): unequal accessibility or crowd burdens
- \\(U\_{\text{model}}\\): uncertainty in the prediction

These are proposed modeling objectives, not validated physical risk equations. The weights and constraints must be defined, tested and reviewed.

